Q Fever Fatigue Syndrome QFS
Complex Fatigue Entities
Post-infection exhaustion post C. burnetii exposure

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-044
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe physical collapse, paralyzing myalgia, and profound cognitive failure following a mild stressor.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute, severe physical crash rendering the patient completely unable to communicate effectively or stand. Immediate physical assessment was mandated to definitively rule out acute neurological events (stroke), severe electrolyte derangement, or recurrent acute infection, and to safely manage the patient's severe distress in the home environment prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My body feels like lead, my brain has stopped working entirely, and I am in constant, deep muscle pain.
History of Present Illness: Patient, an agricultural worker from a rural background, reports a severe exacerbation of a chronic post-infectious state following documented acute Q Fever (C. burnetii) infection 2 years ago. The current crash was triggered by over-exertion two days prior. Symptoms feature profound, paralytic fatigue, severe deep myalgia without obvious joint swelling, and significant cognitive dysfunction (brain fog, slowed speech). The patient describes the fatigue as entirely unresponsive to rest or sleep.
Functional Status: Strictly bedbound tonight; requires physical assistance from spouse to turn over in bed or drink fluids.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of dehydration due to extreme lethargy; patient is highly distressed by the total loss of autonomy.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 68 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 14 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 84 Kg
General: Profoundly lethargic, speaking in a slow, whispered monotone, moving limbs with visible extreme effort.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular, respiratory, and abdominal exams are entirely unremarkable. Neurological exam reveals globally intact cranial nerves and normal (though weak-appearing due to fatigue) symmetric motor tone and reflexes. No focal neurological deficits indicating cerebrovascular accident. Marked, diffuse tenderness to deep palpation across all major muscle compartments (quadriceps, deltoids). Clinical picture represents severe post-infection exhaustion post C. burnetii exposure.
Environmental Observations: Room is heavily darkened and silent to minimize sensory demand.
Medication Reconciliation: No new medications. Uses paracetamol for myalgia with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Q Fever Fatigue Syndrome QFS - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical, global physical weakness and slowing rather than unilateral, focal neurological deficits (e.g., no facial droop, no unilateral arm drift).

Acute Relapse of C. burnetii (Chronic Q Fever Endocarditis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, normal cardiac auscultation (no new murmurs), and normal peripheral perfusion, though formal echocardiogram should be part of long-term care.

Severe Major Depressive Episode - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear timeline linking physical over-exertion to the physiological crash, with prominent, objective physical myalgia driving the presentation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of near-catatonic fatigue and cognitive failure required an immediate, face-to-face home assessment to explicitly rule out life-threatening neurological emergencies such as stroke, or an acute cardiac complication of chronic Q Fever. Having confirmed stable hemodynamics, an intact nervous system, and clear lungs/heart, it was determined the patient is suffering a profound cellular energy crash characteristic of QFS. Transporting this patient to an emergency department would have catastrophically exacerbated their sensory and metabolic exhaustion; therefore, aggressive stabilization and radical rest in the home were prescribed.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed absolute sensory and physical rest (radical resting protocol). Targeted intervention strategy includes LDN, NAD+, Zinc, and Vitamin C to be optimized upon baseline recovery. Instructed spouse on safe, low-exertion oral hydration protocols using electrolyte solutions.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly maintaining total sensory deprivation (eye mask, earplugs, zero conversation) for the next 24 hours, you will actively shut down your brain's energy consumption, directly allowing your cellular batteries to begin recharging and rewarding yourself with a faster exit from the crash.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Bedpan provided for immediate use to eliminate the massive energy cost of bathroom transfers.
Follow-up: Telehealth GP review in 7 days (in-person travel contraindicated).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if the patient becomes completely unrousable to pain, develops sudden severe shortness of breath, or if breathing becomes extremely shallow and erratic.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Q Fever Fatigue Syndrome is a profound metabolic and neuro-immune dysfunction; acute medical intervention focuses solely on supportive care and preventing further energy drain.
Clinical Course: This severe post-exertional crash will likely persist for days to weeks. Recovery of baseline function requires uncompromising, prolonged rest.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Spouse must call 000 immediately if the patient stops breathing normally, becomes completely unresponsive to physical shaking, or develops a high fever.

